1
00:00:01,961 --> 00:00:17,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've been saying many times that the problem is that we are affected in the first place.

2
00:00:19,228 --> 00:00:26,831
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, we are... anger is possible, lust is possible, we can get confused.

3
00:00:27,112 --> 00:00:27,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is possible.

4
00:00:27,952 --> 00:00:29,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is possible for us.

5
00:00:35,318 --> 00:00:37,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you...

6
00:00:37,779 --> 00:00:44,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So the goal, really, is to not be affected in the first place.

7
00:00:44,923 --> 00:00:46,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to do something unaccounted for.

8
00:00:46,844 --> 00:00:47,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

9
00:00:48,164 --> 00:00:57,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like, that's the kind of fundamental difference that I often try to highlight, is dhamma, the practice of dhamma,

10
00:00:58,630 --> 00:01:06,151
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not supposed to just help you with things, help you with things that bother you, help you deal with them.

11
00:01:06,171 --> 00:01:11,892
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically supposed to uproot your liability to it, to things that bother you.

12
00:01:12,633 --> 00:01:14,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't manage your suffering.

13
00:01:14,753 --> 00:01:18,314
[SPEAKER_00]: It removes possibility for you to suffer in the first place.

14
00:01:21,514 --> 00:01:25,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you... and that is a personal goal for your practice.

15
00:01:25,635 --> 00:01:26,195
[SPEAKER_00]: It should be

16
00:01:28,582 --> 00:01:33,026
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to be affected by things to begin with.

17
00:01:33,046 --> 00:01:41,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, I want to have a perfect management technique that will always help me deal with whatever suffering have arisen.

18
00:01:41,373 --> 00:01:42,494
[SPEAKER_00]: See, that's the problem already.

19
00:01:43,195 --> 00:01:44,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering has arisen.

20
00:01:44,796 --> 00:01:50,241
[SPEAKER_00]: You might be very skilled in dealing with it, but you're not skilled in not having it arise in the first place.

21
00:01:50,721 --> 00:01:52,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what should be your issue.

22
00:01:52,843 --> 00:02:03,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, you know, the Buddha would often describe, like, his teaching, that frees you from suffering, brings cessation of being.

23
00:02:03,752 --> 00:02:11,659
[SPEAKER_00]: The ending of bhāva, the ending of these things becoming for you, manifesting, enduring, that you have to manage them later.

24
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Means they're not anymore.

25
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:14,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Being has ceased.

26
00:02:14,881 --> 00:02:15,402
[SPEAKER_00]: They're gone.

27
00:02:16,823 --> 00:02:18,204
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is the goal of the dhamma.

28
00:02:18,324 --> 00:02:19,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of being.

29
00:02:19,685 --> 00:02:22,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Not management of things that are for you, here and now.

30
00:02:24,788 --> 00:02:28,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation of...

31
00:02:28,230 --> 00:02:28,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering.

32
00:02:29,571 --> 00:02:31,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation of greed, aversion, delusion.

33
00:02:34,754 --> 00:02:37,916
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can get angry.

34
00:02:38,936 --> 00:02:39,937
[SPEAKER_01]: I can get angry.

35
00:02:40,557 --> 00:02:45,060
[SPEAKER_01]: But then I can find out some sort of technique to manage my anger.

36
00:02:46,017 --> 00:02:48,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, oh, when you're angry, think these thoughts.

37
00:02:49,019 --> 00:02:50,080
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're angry, do this.

38
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:51,681
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're angry, do that.

39
00:02:52,322 --> 00:02:53,162
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you start.

40
00:02:53,522 --> 00:02:57,625
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be... well, you need to want to be free from anger to begin with.

41
00:02:59,087 --> 00:03:06,492
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, inevitably, your first effort will be directed at how to manage and subdue the anger.

42
00:03:07,684 --> 00:03:14,329
[SPEAKER_00]: But you mustn't confuse that for the actual practice of the dhamma, which, if done rightly, will uproot the anger.

43
00:03:14,449 --> 00:03:16,390
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, there's nothing for you to manage.

44
00:03:16,410 --> 00:03:18,451
[SPEAKER_00]: No burden for you to have to do or deal with.

45
00:03:19,272 --> 00:03:21,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't do it, you will then suffer.

46
00:03:21,594 --> 00:03:21,714
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

47
00:03:21,754 --> 00:03:25,576
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing to be dealt with, because nothing affects you to begin with.

48
00:03:29,219 --> 00:03:30,600
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, it's not the dhamma.

49
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:32,321
[SPEAKER_01]: That management is not the dhamma.

50
00:03:33,259 --> 00:03:33,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

51
00:03:33,639 --> 00:03:36,040
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the approximation of the dhamma.

52
00:03:36,060 --> 00:03:38,521
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not the direct way.

53
00:03:39,081 --> 00:03:50,164
[SPEAKER_00]: One way only, the way the Buddha would describe the dhamma, would always only result in purification of being, like removing all the unwholesomeness and making bhāva cease.

54
00:03:51,365 --> 00:03:52,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Management doesn't do that.

55
00:03:53,565 --> 00:04:01,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Only that direct insight into what uproots suffering, what is uprooting the greed, what is uprooting the aversion and delusion.

56
00:04:06,621 --> 00:04:08,725
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the dhamma?

57
00:04:08,865 --> 00:04:13,333
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a direct path for uprooting, not the management.

58
00:04:14,636 --> 00:04:19,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are kind of responsible for whether things you're doing, whether you've taken them as dhamma,

59
00:04:20,851 --> 00:04:36,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you've taken them for the purpose of uprooting their issues, not dealing with it, but, like, making yourself not liable to them, even in the future, or have you taken the dhamma instruction as a means, sort of, of helping yourself deal with these things.

60
00:04:37,665 --> 00:04:46,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's more like... it's determined by you, by your attitude, whether that instruction will be taken as the dhamma or as management.

61
00:04:50,897 --> 00:04:52,579
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the difference I'm pointing at.

62
00:04:53,360 --> 00:05:02,690
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like, you can do satipaṭṭhāna, which is the dhamma, the way the Buddha described, but you can do it for the reasons of managing your suffering, not for the reasons of uprooting it.

63
00:05:05,853 --> 00:05:09,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you are responsible for whether it's management or it's the dhamma.

64
00:05:09,917 --> 00:05:12,380
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, that's why the dhamma arises in those who see,

65
00:05:14,011 --> 00:05:16,133
[SPEAKER_00]: I can, like, you can tell the dhamma directly.

66
00:05:16,193 --> 00:05:20,738
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha would teach dhamma to somebody, but it wouldn't be the dhamma for them, because they're not understanding it.

67
00:05:20,898 --> 00:05:22,920
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no arising of dhamma in their experience.

68
00:05:24,021 --> 00:05:27,545
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is the thing the Buddha said, but they're not understanding it.

69
00:05:27,565 --> 00:05:29,847
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the dhamma can only arise on that individual level.

70
00:05:30,548 --> 00:05:33,471
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma, the way, knowing the knowledge of the way.

71
00:05:33,972 --> 00:05:36,514
[SPEAKER_00]: The knowledge of the way of uprooting things, basically.

72
00:05:36,534 --> 00:05:37,095
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it is.

73
00:05:38,445 --> 00:05:45,467
[SPEAKER_00]: The knowledge of overcoming liability to suffering once and for all, not just the knowledge of how to manage it skillfully when it arises.

74
00:05:58,131 --> 00:06:03,293
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can look and see I am subject to suffering.

75
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:06,101
[SPEAKER_01]: I will.

76
00:06:07,001 --> 00:06:08,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Things can go wrong.

77
00:06:08,162 --> 00:06:09,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

78
00:06:09,503 --> 00:06:11,184
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really on that level of I will.

79
00:06:11,844 --> 00:06:12,845
[SPEAKER_00]: I will experience this.

80
00:06:12,985 --> 00:06:14,146
[SPEAKER_00]: I am liable to this.

81
00:06:14,906 --> 00:06:15,767
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... we often say that.

82
00:06:15,787 --> 00:06:17,568
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, we say the wise man will reflect.

83
00:06:17,588 --> 00:06:18,809
[SPEAKER_00]: The young man who leaves the home...

84
00:06:19,169 --> 00:06:20,490
[SPEAKER_00]: I am prey to suffering.

85
00:06:21,310 --> 00:06:25,291
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily suffering now, but the suffering will catch me at some point.

86
00:06:25,691 --> 00:06:26,512
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inevitable.

87
00:06:26,892 --> 00:06:37,055
[SPEAKER_00]: And you should start... you should have the, like, mental, basically, clarity enough, mental capacity sufficiently enough, to start seeing that as the actual root of the problem.

88
00:06:38,503 --> 00:06:42,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the fact that you might feel like, oh, I suffer now, I don't suffer tomorrow, or whatever.

89
00:06:42,346 --> 00:06:44,148
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact is that I will at some point.

90
00:06:44,268 --> 00:06:45,048
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inevitable.

91
00:06:45,208 --> 00:06:46,510
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the true problem.

92
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:53,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if I spend the rest of my life skillfully avoiding all the major sufferings, the fact is, it will still have to...

93
00:06:53,255 --> 00:06:54,816
[SPEAKER_00]: I am still liable to it.

94
00:06:55,517 --> 00:06:57,679
[SPEAKER_00]: I still have to make the effort to avoid it.

95
00:06:58,139 --> 00:06:59,220
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already the issue.

96
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:06,726
[SPEAKER_00]: The divine messenger, you know, the Buddha was neither sick, nor old, nor dying, when he saw a sick, aging, dead person.

97
00:07:08,713 --> 00:07:15,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So you see that, ah, this is directly present in my experience as the inherent liability.

98
00:07:16,199 --> 00:07:17,299
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the issue is.

99
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:30,229
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the difference in practice of management and the practice of uprooting, let's call it that way, is basically in recognizing, like, okay, so there is no lust in my experience here and now.

100
00:07:30,790 --> 00:07:32,951
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no greed in my experience here and now.

101
00:07:33,011 --> 00:07:34,632
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no delusion in my experience here and now.

102
00:07:34,652 --> 00:07:35,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I'm fine then.

103
00:07:35,593 --> 00:07:35,733
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

104
00:07:36,214 --> 00:07:40,217
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, as the Buddha would say, okay, so you ask yourself further.

105
00:07:40,777 --> 00:07:45,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It's good that those things are not present here and now, but your next question should now be...

106
00:07:46,301 --> 00:07:51,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is it possible for non-arisen lust to arise later on?

107
00:07:51,945 --> 00:07:54,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Now it's not here, but can it be here tomorrow?

108
00:07:54,307 --> 00:07:55,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, have I addressed that?

109
00:07:56,469 --> 00:07:57,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Here and now.

110
00:07:57,290 --> 00:07:59,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Not wait for tomorrow to address it.

111
00:08:00,212 --> 00:08:01,693
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's literally the crossroads.

112
00:08:02,747 --> 00:08:05,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Between... Are you heading in the direction of uprooting it?

113
00:08:06,289 --> 00:08:07,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you heading in the direction of management?

114
00:08:08,270 --> 00:08:10,251
[SPEAKER_00]: And most people go down the path of management.

115
00:08:10,571 --> 00:08:12,332
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I'll deal with it when it arises.

116
00:08:13,073 --> 00:08:19,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to understand that liability to future lust is a risen issue now.

117
00:08:21,057 --> 00:08:22,838
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to wait for it to arise.

118
00:08:23,258 --> 00:08:30,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a chance for non-manifested lust, non-manifested aversion to manifest in my experience later?

119
00:08:31,528 --> 00:08:33,550
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not sure, means there is a chance.

120
00:08:34,351 --> 00:08:36,753
[SPEAKER_00]: If you know for sure, there you go, you confirmed it.

121
00:08:36,813 --> 00:08:38,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a chance that these things will arise.

122
00:08:39,336 --> 00:08:44,681
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the issue for me now, when those things are not present.

123
00:08:45,442 --> 00:08:47,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is a possibility.

124
00:08:48,994 --> 00:09:03,714
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's the whole... that's like the... what differentiates your practice from... are you aiming at the uprooting or are you just aiming at the skillful management?

125
00:09:05,255 --> 00:09:06,936
[SPEAKER_00]: And you heard that yourself before.

126
00:09:06,976 --> 00:09:15,481
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a very common attitude of, oh, well, you know, most of the time you are not lustful, most of the time you're not angry.

127
00:09:15,541 --> 00:09:17,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, you're doing fine.

128
00:09:17,542 --> 00:09:25,287
[SPEAKER_00]: If most of the time you're not angry, it means anger is still your liability, your possibility, which means you're as angry.

129
00:09:26,359 --> 00:09:31,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're not acting out of that anger as often as others, but that's just because you learned some management of your anger.

130
00:09:32,503 --> 00:09:39,008
[SPEAKER_00]: You're certainly not uprooting anything, if you have the attitude that, most of the time, these things don't arise for me.

131
00:09:39,969 --> 00:09:42,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but could they arise at any time?

132
00:09:42,731 --> 00:09:42,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

133
00:09:43,552 --> 00:09:46,514
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the issue you're not seeing, and that's why you're not uprooting anything.

134
00:09:54,043 --> 00:10:00,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see that there is a problem, because you see and know that there is the possibility of anger.

135
00:10:00,548 --> 00:10:00,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

136
00:10:00,708 --> 00:10:06,432
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to train your mind to start seeing these possibilities as actually present, because they are.

137
00:10:06,753 --> 00:10:12,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can think to yourself, I can manage my lust or my anger quite well.

138
00:10:13,538 --> 00:10:15,239
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm really skilled at that.

139
00:10:16,248 --> 00:10:25,277
[SPEAKER_01]: I know all these different techniques, psychological methods to employ, so that I can ease that anger, ease that lust, and be calm again.

140
00:10:26,158 --> 00:10:31,142
[SPEAKER_00]: If your way out of suffering is skillful managing it, you need suffering to arise first.

141
00:10:32,864 --> 00:10:35,727
[SPEAKER_00]: So, management requires suffering in order to manage it.

142
00:10:36,427 --> 00:10:38,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So, management cannot free you from that suffering.

143
00:10:39,468 --> 00:10:42,529
[SPEAKER_00]: It can only deal with it once it has arisen.

144
00:10:42,749 --> 00:10:53,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And most people, that's the that's the kind of problem that's sort of relatively subtle, I suppose, is people, as I said, natural tendency is to turn towards management first.

145
00:10:53,552 --> 00:10:56,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, they get rid of the problem, do it skillfully, whatever else.

146
00:10:58,154 --> 00:11:02,235
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is this kind of conflating that occurs then, or rather confusion.

147
00:11:02,875 --> 00:11:08,297
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through your management of suffering, you experience like less dukkha,

148
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:13,939
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, at the same time, you kind of start...

149
00:11:14,835 --> 00:11:26,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, tacitly assuming, hoping that all you have to do is manage your suffering long enough, and then somehow, magically, liability to dukkha will disappear.

150
00:11:27,858 --> 00:11:41,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all I have to do is adhere to this management and skillful avoiding and so on, and there's already implicit, as I said, wishful thinking that implies that you hope for suffering will then just poof, eventually.

151
00:11:41,781 --> 00:11:43,482
[SPEAKER_00]: From your management, it will just evaporate.

152
00:11:44,082 --> 00:11:44,863
[SPEAKER_00]: But it won't.

153
00:11:45,223 --> 00:11:45,824
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

154
00:11:46,564 --> 00:11:50,128
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way that suffering will evaporate is if you evaporate it.

155
00:11:50,928 --> 00:11:52,270
[SPEAKER_00]: If you understand the core.

156
00:11:52,290 --> 00:11:53,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Pull the thorn out.

157
00:11:56,233 --> 00:11:57,695
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't come out by itself.

158
00:11:58,395 --> 00:11:59,476
[SPEAKER_00]: So, uproot it.

159
00:12:02,599 --> 00:12:09,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you keep managing it with a hope that somehow it will disappear, on the level of that I am still liable to it,

160
00:12:11,059 --> 00:12:13,141
[SPEAKER_00]: That means your practice revolves around wishful thinking.

161
00:12:13,721 --> 00:12:15,202
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no direct insight.

162
00:12:15,763 --> 00:12:18,645
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what to be done for freedom from suffering.

163
00:12:18,805 --> 00:12:23,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, you don't want... Everyone's goal is to not have anger around.

164
00:12:23,529 --> 00:12:24,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

165
00:12:24,290 --> 00:12:31,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody wants to be fully free from suffering, which means everybody wants to not even be liable to discomfort and displeasure.

166
00:12:33,117 --> 00:12:36,941
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I start doing metta, metta, metta, metta, metta, every day, every day, every day.

167
00:12:37,241 --> 00:12:38,082
[SPEAKER_01]: And I stay calm.

168
00:12:38,522 --> 00:12:39,263
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't get angry.

169
00:12:40,630 --> 00:12:42,611
[SPEAKER_01]: But I have to keep doing methods, because I can't...

170
00:12:42,671 --> 00:12:51,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your calmness then is dependent upon the factor of you sustaining your effort towards whatever you perceive metacontemplation to be.

171
00:12:52,414 --> 00:13:07,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, if sometimes circumstances change, you get sick, or something happens, or too many things happen, you don't have enough time, so you cannot engage to that necessary extent of your metacontemplation, means you're back within anger.

172
00:13:08,181 --> 00:13:13,147
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you haven't really uprooted anger, you just suppressed it with certain practices.

173
00:13:14,509 --> 00:13:25,502
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, there is not... it's not inherently wrong to do that, to begin with, but it's inherently wrong to adopt the view that doing that uproots things somehow magically.

174
00:13:26,406 --> 00:13:30,248
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to uproot things is to directly see what uprooting is.

175
00:13:31,088 --> 00:13:32,809
[SPEAKER_00]: And I just described it, what it is.

176
00:13:32,949 --> 00:13:45,796
[SPEAKER_00]: It's recognizing that liability, the possibility of anger, possibility of ill-will, possibility of sensuality, possibility of delusion, possibility of dukkha, as the dukkha right now.

177
00:13:45,836 --> 00:13:47,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to wait for it when it occurs.

178
00:13:49,612 --> 00:13:51,593
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see that possibility.

179
00:13:51,893 --> 00:13:52,553
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you do?

180
00:13:53,334 --> 00:13:57,696
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you uproot this possibility that tomorrow you might suffer?

181
00:13:57,716 --> 00:14:06,400
[SPEAKER_01]: You're being aware of that, but then... Well, it's sustaining that.

182
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:09,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can you sustain that if you're not keeping the precepts?

183
00:14:10,002 --> 00:14:16,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you sustain that elevated state of mind that recognizes liability to dukkha without the actual dukkha being present there?

184
00:14:17,698 --> 00:14:23,283
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're drinking, smoking, running around, distracting yourself, engaging in entertainment, no, impossible.

185
00:14:24,364 --> 00:14:26,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So that the noble eightfold path, that's how you do it.

186
00:14:26,746 --> 00:14:29,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Right livelihood, right view, first, first of all.

187
00:14:30,089 --> 00:14:38,235
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if your view still regards management as the way out of suffering, you will not stay on the level of weight.

188
00:14:38,696 --> 00:14:43,760
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the possibility of suffering, that's the problem there must be dealt with, not the actual suffering when it arises.

189
00:14:45,232 --> 00:14:46,732
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, how do I deal with that?

190
00:14:47,293 --> 00:14:48,033
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you deal with what?

191
00:14:48,133 --> 00:14:48,913
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility.

192
00:14:49,153 --> 00:14:50,293
[SPEAKER_01]: But no belief or path.

193
00:14:50,353 --> 00:14:51,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

194
00:14:51,554 --> 00:15:04,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, right view, knowing that that's where the issue is, not looking elsewhere, right intentions, not entertaining, not delighting, not giving in to greed, aversion, delusion through your speech, through your body, in your thoughts.

195
00:15:06,731 --> 00:15:14,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on every front, you need to build yourself up, in order to be able to sustain that recognition of liability here and now.

196
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:15,541
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what the issue is.

197
00:15:15,841 --> 00:15:21,448
[SPEAKER_00]: If you ask yourself, how do I deal with the possibility of future suffering here and now?

198
00:15:21,888 --> 00:15:23,490
[SPEAKER_00]: You're basically asking, how do I manage it?

199
00:15:25,223 --> 00:15:26,343
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I manage this possibility?

200
00:15:26,363 --> 00:15:27,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what you're implying.

201
00:15:27,624 --> 00:15:29,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you want to deal with it now.

202
00:15:29,744 --> 00:15:37,386
[SPEAKER_00]: You deal with it properly, not... not give in to the view of management, by realizing that you have no right to resist it.

203
00:15:38,006 --> 00:15:47,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to be careful if you... when you start recognizing, ah, the liability of suffering is the problem for me, you have to be careful to now not give in to the attitude of wanting to manage that.

204
00:15:49,478 --> 00:15:53,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want it to end.

205
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:56,381
[SPEAKER_01]: I want that possibility to be gone.

206
00:15:57,521 --> 00:16:02,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you really have to understand where the dukkha is there.

207
00:16:03,463 --> 00:16:05,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the possibility in itself dukkha?

208
00:16:06,424 --> 00:16:13,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that possibility of future discomfort, future disagreeable things, is that in itself dukkha?

209
00:16:14,267 --> 00:16:14,748
[SPEAKER_01]: The idea.

210
00:16:15,508 --> 00:16:16,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the thought of it.

211
00:16:16,948 --> 00:16:18,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that the dukkha?

212
00:16:20,147 --> 00:16:22,088
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's the dukkha then?

213
00:16:22,428 --> 00:16:24,069
[SPEAKER_01]: I would say it's that unpleasant feeling.

214
00:16:24,989 --> 00:16:30,131
[SPEAKER_01]: It's this pleasure that is there when I have that idea.

215
00:16:31,431 --> 00:16:31,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

216
00:16:32,272 --> 00:16:43,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I feel uncomfortable with the fact that in the future I will suffer pain which I'm suffering presently because of that.

217
00:16:44,256 --> 00:16:48,138
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the next question you should ask yourself is the

218
00:16:50,124 --> 00:16:56,510
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the unpleasant feeling then, that you have when you think those thoughts, is that the dukkha in itself?

219
00:16:57,250 --> 00:16:58,471
No, no, no.

220
00:16:59,772 --> 00:17:02,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is what it is, that pain.

221
00:17:03,315 --> 00:17:03,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

222
00:17:03,896 --> 00:17:06,638
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, I don't want it there.

223
00:17:06,878 --> 00:17:08,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is then the dukkha?

224
00:17:11,562 --> 00:17:14,205
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I would say, okay, it's the craving to get rid of it.

225
00:17:15,497 --> 00:17:15,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

226
00:17:15,877 --> 00:17:17,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how is that manifested?

227
00:17:17,098 --> 00:17:17,579
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that?

228
00:17:17,659 --> 00:17:20,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically, in your experience... Resistance.

229
00:17:20,561 --> 00:17:20,921
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

230
00:17:21,182 --> 00:17:21,802
[SPEAKER_01]: I resist that...

231
00:17:21,822 --> 00:17:23,704
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's for me that sense of entitlement of...

232
00:17:24,404 --> 00:17:25,185
[SPEAKER_00]: I should not have this.

233
00:17:25,825 --> 00:17:32,631
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, you suffer because you resist the discomfort, even before the discomfort comes your way.

234
00:17:33,131 --> 00:17:40,818
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you resist the thought of the possibility of discomfort here and now, and that's why here and now, you experience dukkha.

235
00:17:41,357 --> 00:17:44,525
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha didn't say vedanā is the cause of dukkha.

236
00:17:44,665 --> 00:17:47,131
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't say feelings are the cause of suffering.

237
00:17:47,833 --> 00:17:50,780
[SPEAKER_00]: He said taṇha, craving, is the cause of suffering.

238
00:17:51,495 --> 00:17:53,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving in regard to what you feel.

239
00:17:53,836 --> 00:18:05,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have such and such feeling when you have such and such thought, present, future or past, but it's your implicit attitude towards the feeling of either craving towards it or away from it.

240
00:18:05,858 --> 00:18:08,639
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that experience is dukkha.

241
00:18:08,859 --> 00:18:12,860
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, you would have not seen that if you haven't forced yourself

242
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:18,101
[SPEAKER_00]: To discern the level of that possibility of dukkha, is dukkha right now.

243
00:18:18,681 --> 00:18:28,204
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't have seen that if you were just fully bent on managing the dukkha as it arises, or managing the dukkha before it arises.

244
00:18:28,224 --> 00:18:29,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Specific pain.

245
00:18:29,324 --> 00:18:29,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

246
00:18:29,644 --> 00:18:30,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

247
00:18:30,705 --> 00:18:39,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even when people recognize sometimes the future dukkha, liability to the future dukkha, they can even take that as something to manage.

248
00:18:39,267 --> 00:18:42,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, so I'll do this, I'll do this, so these things will not happen to me.

249
00:18:43,085 --> 00:18:53,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Which carries the implicit view that dukkha is in those actual things, or in your actual feelings, and you don't see that it's in your attitude towards these things.

250
00:18:53,712 --> 00:18:54,812
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, it's right now.

251
00:18:55,092 --> 00:18:55,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Right here, right now.

252
00:18:56,033 --> 00:18:56,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Always present.

253
00:18:57,054 --> 00:18:57,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

254
00:18:57,494 --> 00:18:59,015
[SPEAKER_01]: You're experiencing that pressure.

255
00:18:59,415 --> 00:19:04,859
[SPEAKER_00]: The relationship between taṇhā and dukkha, between craving and suffering, it's always present.

256
00:19:05,919 --> 00:19:07,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Always simultaneously present.

257
00:19:07,700 --> 00:19:08,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of suffering...

258
00:19:08,941 --> 00:19:11,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of suffering means presence of taṇhā.

259
00:19:13,002 --> 00:19:15,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of taṇha means presence of suffering.

260
00:19:17,523 --> 00:19:23,604
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to the extent that taṇha is present, to that extent suffering is felt.

261
00:19:23,624 --> 00:19:28,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, to the extent that suffering is felt, to that extent attitude of taṇha is present.

262
00:19:28,325 --> 00:19:33,247
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no delay, or like few seconds later dukkha comes as a result of your craving.

263
00:19:34,627 --> 00:19:34,767
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

264
00:19:34,887 --> 00:19:37,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of suffering means presence of craving.

265
00:19:37,908 --> 00:19:39,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering.

266
00:19:40,845 --> 00:19:54,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why if you were to remove the presence of craving, there is no suffering amidst things that used to cause you to suffer so much.

267
00:19:54,389 --> 00:19:58,410
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why liberation is visible and possible from suffering here and now.

268
00:19:58,990 --> 00:19:59,511
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what I mean.

269
00:19:59,531 --> 00:20:07,453
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to start seeing as dukkha the liability to suffering, not actually wait for the actual suffering and then manage it.

270
00:20:11,268 --> 00:20:14,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't see that liability, then you're complacent.

271
00:20:15,450 --> 00:20:20,151
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not seeing the liability, means you are not seeing where your craving is.

272
00:20:20,191 --> 00:20:21,032
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you're not seeing dukkha.

273
00:20:21,232 --> 00:20:21,912
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing dukkha.

274
00:20:21,932 --> 00:20:22,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

275
00:20:22,572 --> 00:20:25,913
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see the dukkha first, in order to see the cause of dukkha.

276
00:20:25,953 --> 00:20:29,395
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just be dealing with the dukkha without understanding what dukkha is.

277
00:20:29,435 --> 00:20:30,015
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I said.

278
00:20:30,375 --> 00:20:32,916
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm teaching suffering and I'm teaching freedom.

279
00:20:32,956 --> 00:20:33,856
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm teaching only two things.

280
00:20:33,896 --> 00:20:35,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering and freedom from it.

281
00:20:35,497 --> 00:20:35,857
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

282
00:20:37,795 --> 00:20:44,117
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't see that possibility of future discomfort is already dukkha now, means you're not seeing the nature of dukkha to begin with.

283
00:20:45,497 --> 00:20:49,878
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter what happens to you, you will not see it with the correct set of eyes, so to speak.

284
00:20:56,640 --> 00:21:02,622
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're not seeing what the dukkha is here and now, already, you're not gonna see your craving.

285
00:21:03,651 --> 00:21:07,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, to the extent that the dukkha is felt, to that extent craving is present.

286
00:21:07,893 --> 00:21:11,835
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not seeing the felt dukkha, thus how can you see that which determines it?

287
00:21:12,035 --> 00:21:12,856
[SPEAKER_00]: The actual craving.

288
00:21:13,696 --> 00:21:14,256
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

289
00:21:14,517 --> 00:21:20,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Management, looking to deal with it, over there, without seeing where the actual dukkha is,

290
00:21:21,623 --> 00:21:29,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you give in to that need to manage it and remove the pain, as opposed to understand it, it's... it's... it's inherently bound in the wrong view.

291
00:21:30,269 --> 00:21:32,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inherently bound in the wrong direction.

292
00:21:33,012 --> 00:21:34,413
[SPEAKER_00]: There's just like a way you could reflect.

293
00:21:34,753 --> 00:21:37,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you entitled to... to... to manage pain?

294
00:21:38,295 --> 00:21:42,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Have you had saying it's a... like dwell on the nature of that dukkha?

295
00:21:43,499 --> 00:21:45,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the actual dukkha or the future dukkha.

296
00:21:45,281 --> 00:21:46,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Just... just reflect it.

297
00:21:46,722 --> 00:21:48,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Have you had saying it's arising?

298
00:21:49,686 --> 00:21:54,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you prevent circumstances from becoming disagreeable at any point in the future?

299
00:21:54,689 --> 00:21:57,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that even conceivable, actually?

300
00:21:57,211 --> 00:21:58,352
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a pure fantasy.

301
00:21:59,472 --> 00:22:03,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Fantasy rooted in this complete misconceiving of the problem.

302
00:22:03,675 --> 00:22:06,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Problem is in these things happening to me.

303
00:22:06,137 --> 00:22:11,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Problem is not in me implicitly resisting the idea of these things happening to me.

304
00:22:12,040 --> 00:22:14,322
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you stop resisting the pain?

305
00:22:16,720 --> 00:22:19,961
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you then stop craving, or rather welcoming the pleasure?

306
00:22:21,182 --> 00:22:22,382
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you remove craving?

307
00:22:23,143 --> 00:22:25,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we just outlined it.

308
00:22:25,824 --> 00:22:33,287
[SPEAKER_00]: By not trying to manage it, but by seeing it here and now already.

309
00:22:35,048 --> 00:22:36,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And then by sense restraint.

310
00:22:36,308 --> 00:22:42,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you cannot possibly be welcoming pleasure, and then just dealing with the unpleasant experiences when they arise.

311
00:22:42,691 --> 00:22:45,332
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just selectively pick and choose which cravings you want.

312
00:22:47,506 --> 00:22:51,167
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is any form of craving present, means liability to suffering is present.

313
00:22:52,407 --> 00:22:56,548
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to abandon craving for pleasure, inasmuch you have to abandon craving against pain.

314
00:22:57,048 --> 00:22:58,789
[SPEAKER_01]: So, pain comes, pleasure comes.

315
00:22:59,549 --> 00:23:00,909
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not saying it's arising.

316
00:23:01,969 --> 00:23:10,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you then constantly entertain the attitude of, I want this, I don't want this.

317
00:23:10,651 --> 00:23:13,012
[SPEAKER_00]: I have right to want this, I have right to not want this.

318
00:23:14,908 --> 00:23:17,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you go after one, try to avoid the other one?

319
00:23:17,912 --> 00:23:20,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is your attitude rooted?

320
00:23:20,896 --> 00:23:21,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you doing it?

321
00:23:23,480 --> 00:23:27,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's... because pleasure and pain is... yeah, it's felt.

322
00:23:27,585 --> 00:23:28,407
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a pressure.

323
00:23:28,607 --> 00:23:29,708
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pressures you feel.

324
00:23:31,195 --> 00:23:32,516
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's painful to not do it.

325
00:23:33,076 --> 00:23:33,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

326
00:23:33,436 --> 00:23:35,057
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you do it because you want to avoid pain.

327
00:23:35,638 --> 00:23:36,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's pressuring.

328
00:23:36,718 --> 00:23:37,338
[SPEAKER_01]: The pain is...

329
00:23:37,559 --> 00:23:38,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Pain is pressuring.

330
00:23:38,339 --> 00:23:38,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So, exactly.

331
00:23:39,019 --> 00:23:40,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't know how to deal with dukkha.

332
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:42,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you try to get rid of dukkha.

333
00:23:42,762 --> 00:23:46,804
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically the the the the nutshell of every management.

334
00:23:47,644 --> 00:23:49,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I say it's inherently wrong.

335
00:23:50,406 --> 00:23:59,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're doing the dhamma, all the Buddha's instruction, but you're taking them as management, means you still entertain the same attitude because of which you suffer in the first place.

336
00:24:00,217 --> 00:24:02,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is, I can handle this, I want to get rid of it.

337
00:24:03,038 --> 00:24:04,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the attitude of craving.

338
00:24:09,419 --> 00:24:12,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you then withstand the pressure?

339
00:24:12,840 --> 00:24:14,980
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you withstand the pressure?

340
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:17,921
[SPEAKER_01]: By withstanding the pressure.

341
00:24:17,981 --> 00:24:22,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but can you withstand the pressure if even your physical behavior is not kept in check?

342
00:24:23,042 --> 00:24:24,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you withstand mental pressure then?

343
00:24:24,862 --> 00:24:25,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

344
00:24:27,196 --> 00:24:38,640
[SPEAKER_00]: If your verbal behavior is not kept in check, like the outpours of lust and anger through your physical action, through your verbal action, if you don't keep a lid on that, can you possibly withstand things mentally?

345
00:24:38,760 --> 00:24:39,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Not a chance.

346
00:24:40,121 --> 00:24:41,001
[SPEAKER_00]: And how do you do that?

347
00:24:41,201 --> 00:24:41,902
[SPEAKER_00]: By doing it.

348
00:24:42,882 --> 00:24:44,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how do you become stronger?

349
00:24:44,323 --> 00:24:47,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by exposing yourself to things that will make you stronger.

350
00:24:47,264 --> 00:24:47,824
[SPEAKER_00]: By training.

351
00:24:49,523 --> 00:24:50,284
[SPEAKER_00]: I said that many times.

352
00:24:50,304 --> 00:24:56,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even when I first read Daya Suttas, I was a bit puzzled, but it kept talking about he trains himself thus.

353
00:24:57,708 --> 00:24:59,610
[SPEAKER_00]: A monk, skilled monk, trains himself thus.

354
00:25:00,190 --> 00:25:02,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, you had all these notions about enlightenment and so on.

355
00:25:02,772 --> 00:25:04,133
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, what is there to train?

356
00:25:04,193 --> 00:25:06,494
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you do things and then enlightenment happens to you.

357
00:25:06,554 --> 00:25:06,714
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

358
00:25:07,335 --> 00:25:13,859
[SPEAKER_00]: You train the strength out of which wisdom comes, which is what enlightenment is.

359
00:25:13,879 --> 00:25:15,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why sensory strain is not optional.

360
00:25:16,756 --> 00:25:20,399
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where virtue is not, you know, oh, I can do it for a while.

361
00:25:20,439 --> 00:25:22,722
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a necessary basis that has to remain throughout.

362
00:25:25,584 --> 00:25:31,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will be able to withstand the pressure by not giving in to pressure.

363
00:25:31,690 --> 00:25:35,774
[SPEAKER_00]: By not giving in to pressure, you get to outline what the actual problem is.

364
00:25:36,614 --> 00:25:42,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Your liability to future pressure, to future discomfort, is already a problem right here, right now.

365
00:25:44,626 --> 00:25:50,449
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see that now, because you haven't just blindly gave in to managing of the pressure in order to get rid of the pressure.

366
00:25:52,830 --> 00:25:55,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Manage the stress in order to get rid of the stress.

367
00:25:55,431 --> 00:25:59,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Practice the dhamma, so you don't get this particular stress anymore.

368
00:25:59,854 --> 00:26:03,395
[SPEAKER_01]: I recognize that there's a possibility of suffering.

369
00:26:03,415 --> 00:26:06,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And I see that suffering right now.

370
00:26:06,837 --> 00:26:12,200
[SPEAKER_01]: It's suffering because I resist the displeasure here and now in regard to the possibility.

371
00:26:15,480 --> 00:26:16,801
[SPEAKER_01]: It's suffering because I resist.

372
00:26:19,464 --> 00:26:25,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So I don't resist it, that discomfort here and now.

373
00:26:27,172 --> 00:26:27,973
[SPEAKER_01]: But I must have that...

374
00:26:28,214 --> 00:26:29,335
[SPEAKER_01]: I must still have the idea...

375
00:26:31,157 --> 00:26:37,043
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have to keep on thinking about that there's a possibility of me suffering tomorrow.

376
00:26:37,083 --> 00:26:37,944
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that will come and go.

377
00:26:43,452 --> 00:26:46,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever... basically, I have to look at whatever I'm feeling right now.

378
00:26:47,053 --> 00:26:49,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, because that's where the resistance occurs.

379
00:26:50,514 --> 00:26:51,374
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what craving is.

380
00:26:51,534 --> 00:26:52,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It's where you feel.

381
00:26:52,555 --> 00:26:54,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I can put the possibility of suffering...

382
00:26:54,495 --> 00:26:57,196
[SPEAKER_01]: I put that aside, because now I know what the problem is here and now.

383
00:26:57,336 --> 00:26:58,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

384
00:26:58,396 --> 00:27:00,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I've come to that conclusion.

385
00:27:00,357 --> 00:27:06,339
[SPEAKER_00]: You contemplate the possibility of suffering when you forget what the problem is here and now, or when you're not seeing it.

386
00:27:06,499 --> 00:27:09,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, ask... like Sariputta, just ask himself.

387
00:27:10,120 --> 00:27:10,940
[SPEAKER_01]: I think of Sariputta.

388
00:27:12,146 --> 00:27:12,326
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

389
00:27:12,587 --> 00:27:15,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I liable to anything, to any suffering?

390
00:27:15,211 --> 00:27:16,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it something like that?

391
00:27:16,092 --> 00:27:16,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it was something like that.

392
00:27:16,853 --> 00:27:18,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any suffering that can arise?

393
00:27:18,215 --> 00:27:24,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, is there any suffering that hasn't... that the possibility of suffering hasn't been, like, addressed, so to speak?

394
00:27:24,605 --> 00:27:24,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

395
00:27:24,825 --> 00:27:27,409
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, no, I don't see any.

396
00:27:27,829 --> 00:27:28,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

397
00:27:28,789 --> 00:27:31,151
[SPEAKER_00]: He still doesn't decide, okay, I'm done now.

398
00:27:31,171 --> 00:27:31,311
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

399
00:27:31,411 --> 00:27:33,953
[SPEAKER_00]: You remain with that vigilant attitude.

400
00:27:34,493 --> 00:27:36,014
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't say, okay, I'll check.

401
00:27:36,174 --> 00:27:38,536
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll check in every now and then and see if something has changed.

402
00:27:39,016 --> 00:27:46,521
[SPEAKER_00]: The possibility for lust, aversion, delusion, possibility for pressure, possibility for central pull, possibility for suffering.

403
00:27:47,362 --> 00:27:48,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Has that arisen now?

404
00:27:49,123 --> 00:27:54,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the actual suffering, but the possibility for it being, oh, look, yeah, now I see it.

405
00:27:54,266 --> 00:27:55,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it could, it's possible.

406
00:27:55,984 --> 00:27:56,365
[SPEAKER_00]: There you go.

407
00:27:56,505 --> 00:27:57,046
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your issue.

408
00:27:57,346 --> 00:27:57,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Right there and then.

409
00:27:58,788 --> 00:27:59,429
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you do?

410
00:28:00,630 --> 00:28:02,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you don't lose the sight of it, to begin with.

411
00:28:02,913 --> 00:28:03,494
[SPEAKER_01]: That feeling.

412
00:28:03,694 --> 00:28:03,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

413
00:28:04,175 --> 00:28:10,984
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't welcome it, if it's the possibility towards pleasure and sensuality, and you don't resist it, if it's the possibility towards pain and discomfort.

414
00:28:14,522 --> 00:28:20,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there anything that... is there a possibility for me... indulging in... Welcoming.

415
00:28:21,084 --> 00:28:22,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Indulging is too coarse already.

416
00:28:22,725 --> 00:28:24,506
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the result of pleasure in the future.

417
00:28:24,766 --> 00:28:25,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

418
00:28:25,126 --> 00:28:32,889
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's on the level of the possibility of pleasure or possibility of pain, which means on the level of welcoming or kind of shying away from.

419
00:28:32,909 --> 00:28:33,709
[SPEAKER_00]: It's subtler.

420
00:28:34,329 --> 00:28:39,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think, if someone brings me a pizza tomorrow and I love the taste of pizza, will I

421
00:28:40,778 --> 00:28:43,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I welcome that pleasurable taste?

422
00:28:43,120 --> 00:28:44,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I get lost?

423
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:48,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will know that, if you're looking at things rightly, you will know that already now.

424
00:28:49,324 --> 00:28:49,684
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll know.

425
00:28:49,884 --> 00:28:50,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I can't wait.

426
00:28:51,285 --> 00:29:00,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you are perfectly restrained and don't express your joy over the particular type of food brought to you, you already, internally, you know what it is.

427
00:29:00,832 --> 00:29:03,293
[SPEAKER_01]: And so that... And that's the problem.

428
00:29:03,934 --> 00:29:04,594
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's the work.

429
00:29:05,235 --> 00:29:06,275
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the work, exactly.

430
00:29:06,315 --> 00:29:07,116
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the issue is.

431
00:29:09,252 --> 00:29:14,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And people would... okay, so I will tell them never to bring that thing again, because I like it too much.

432
00:29:15,293 --> 00:29:20,594
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, you can do that as a way of, like, if you really can't deal with things, so okay, put it aside.

433
00:29:21,194 --> 00:29:23,135
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's really masking the issue.

434
00:29:24,195 --> 00:29:27,196
[SPEAKER_00]: The issue is not the pizza or whatever people have brought to you.

435
00:29:27,756 --> 00:29:29,876
[SPEAKER_00]: The issue is you're unable to restrain yourself.

436
00:29:30,536 --> 00:29:33,077
[SPEAKER_00]: You're unable to have mind free from greed.

437
00:29:33,857 --> 00:29:36,377
[SPEAKER_00]: So now you're just trying to remove things that make you greedy.

438
00:29:36,497 --> 00:29:38,998
[SPEAKER_01]: But... You're unable to not be...

439
00:29:40,359 --> 00:29:41,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting pleasure.

440
00:29:41,420 --> 00:29:45,722
[SPEAKER_00]: You are unable to, exactly, to not welcome that pressure of that pleasure.

441
00:29:45,942 --> 00:29:48,483
[SPEAKER_00]: You're unable, which means that's where you will remain liable.

442
00:29:49,203 --> 00:29:52,865
[SPEAKER_00]: You can spend hundred years of that management, you will never uproot your sensuality.

443
00:29:53,665 --> 00:29:59,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you feel the pressure of pain, that resistance against it, and you stop.

444
00:30:00,689 --> 00:30:01,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

445
00:30:01,549 --> 00:30:02,770
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't try to get rid of it,

446
00:30:04,292 --> 00:30:08,635
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't try to psychologize, manage, explain, prevent things from arising.

447
00:30:08,935 --> 00:30:21,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, if something obviously unwholesome, like... like when that man came and offered... wanted to offer his daughter to the Buddha, and we say, no, don't even bother, I will not accept that.

448
00:30:21,624 --> 00:30:25,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Like certain things cannot be done rightly, such as sexual intercourse and so on.

449
00:30:26,447 --> 00:30:30,350
[SPEAKER_00]: But there are things that there's nothing... like there's nothing wrong in eating food, whether it's tasty or not.

450
00:30:32,322 --> 00:30:34,444
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's wrong in eating it with sensual reasons.

451
00:30:35,084 --> 00:30:47,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And you eat... And you eat the... you eat your food with sensual reasons, when... when you are unable to... to not be overwhelmed by that pressure towards accepting pleasure.

452
00:30:52,898 --> 00:30:58,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why Buddha would say, like, when you... when you practice in regard to the food, not like you stop eating,

453
00:30:59,142 --> 00:30:59,322
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

454
00:30:59,682 --> 00:31:00,603
[SPEAKER_00]: You eat mindfully.

455
00:31:01,183 --> 00:31:05,246
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain the reasons for your eating clearly, and you eat while those reasons are present.

456
00:31:05,346 --> 00:31:09,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Reasons for health, sustaining the body, so that you can practice further.

457
00:31:09,389 --> 00:31:12,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Not reasons of beautifications, chasing the taste, and so on.

458
00:31:13,892 --> 00:31:19,756
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you just think, if I avoid the food, I don't have to deal with these reasons, well, you're sorely mistaken.

459
00:31:20,676 --> 00:31:22,938
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just covering up the basis of your centrality.

460
00:31:24,938 --> 00:31:36,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you just eat horrible stuff all the time, because you don't want to experience any sensual joy of the taste, you still need to look at these reasons and keep the vigilant eye on them, because that's where sensuality is rooted.

461
00:31:37,366 --> 00:31:38,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in the actual taste.

462
00:31:42,910 --> 00:31:43,930
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I welcoming?

463
00:31:44,050 --> 00:31:44,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I delighting?

464
00:31:44,991 --> 00:31:50,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I accepting this elation on account of central prospect that is pleasing to me?

465
00:31:51,473 --> 00:31:52,293
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the issue.

466
00:31:52,413 --> 00:31:53,233
[SPEAKER_00]: My acceptance.

467
00:31:53,293 --> 00:31:54,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Not seeing the danger in it.

468
00:31:55,494 --> 00:31:57,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Not... That's the issue.

469
00:31:57,674 --> 00:32:02,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the issue is, oh, I must not have this thing arise for me at all.

470
00:32:04,631 --> 00:32:10,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Call people, tell them never to bring their food, never to... you'll never see anything, you'll never hear anything, you'll never taste anything agreeable.

471
00:32:12,141 --> 00:32:15,125
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're not... you're misconceiving where the issue is.

472
00:32:15,185 --> 00:32:15,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

473
00:32:16,087 --> 00:32:17,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're maintaining the issue.

474
00:32:18,067 --> 00:32:19,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you remain fully within it, yeah.

475
00:32:19,889 --> 00:32:22,611
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you're not stepping outside of central domain.

476
00:32:22,691 --> 00:32:36,385
[SPEAKER_00]: There might be someone that comes along... And that's why the asceticism, the atma's form, like, you know, giants, you know, the perfect practice of obsessive ahimsa, non-violence, not eating, not harming anything, it's utterly misguided.

477
00:32:37,005 --> 00:32:38,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's fully misprojected.

478
00:32:39,628 --> 00:32:41,369
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see where the issue really is.

479
00:32:42,310 --> 00:32:52,116
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, avoiding food, any food, just eating every third day or something, to the point of, in the end, you die on account of starvation, and that's like the ultimate goal of a Jain.

480
00:32:52,656 --> 00:32:57,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you realize you fully remain bound by the central domain, never a step closer to escape it.

481
00:32:58,699 --> 00:33:10,486
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the Jains that said that, by avoiding things, then you diminish the pressure, but...

482
00:33:12,452 --> 00:33:14,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Where's the Buddhist ways?

483
00:33:14,674 --> 00:33:14,894
[SPEAKER_00]: What?

484
00:33:14,994 --> 00:33:16,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not always the same things.

485
00:33:17,036 --> 00:33:19,157
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't always have one attitude towards all things.

486
00:33:19,998 --> 00:33:27,224
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to only have the attitude of accepting everything, every type of feeling, every type of perception, then Jains would have been right.

487
00:33:27,844 --> 00:33:29,706
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is avoid it, because you always accept it.

488
00:33:30,787 --> 00:33:31,928
[SPEAKER_00]: But, but that's not the case.

489
00:33:32,828 --> 00:33:35,811
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not, you're not accepting the the unpleasurable feelings.

490
00:33:36,631 --> 00:33:38,253
[SPEAKER_00]: You are actually trying to get rid of them.

491
00:33:39,214 --> 00:33:40,755
[SPEAKER_00]: You're only accepting certain things.

492
00:33:41,457 --> 00:33:43,438
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you're resisting some other things.

493
00:33:44,719 --> 00:33:46,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, it's not a blanket rule.

494
00:33:46,801 --> 00:33:50,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Always avoid, because it's not a blanket experience.

495
00:33:50,343 --> 00:33:51,144
[SPEAKER_00]: That's always the same.

496
00:33:52,685 --> 00:33:59,990
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, what you do, whether you avoid, or whether you have to basically endure, is determined by how you feel.

497
00:34:00,270 --> 00:34:03,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't even feel that it's always the same in regard to the same things.

498
00:34:04,233 --> 00:34:06,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, one same food might make you happy.

499
00:34:06,374 --> 00:34:07,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, it might make you unpleasant.

500
00:34:08,536 --> 00:34:10,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, it might make you indifferent.

501
00:34:11,700 --> 00:34:13,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you have three different types of feelings.

502
00:34:13,221 --> 00:34:18,162
[SPEAKER_00]: You have three different types of attitude that will then exhibit the craving.

503
00:34:18,863 --> 00:34:24,945
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you always avoid everything, you're ignoring two-thirds of that picture.

504
00:34:25,445 --> 00:34:26,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you will remain ignorant.

505
00:34:29,427 --> 00:34:32,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you then know when to reject and when to accept?

506
00:34:34,049 --> 00:34:37,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when you're resisting gratuitously the future prospect of suffering,

507
00:34:39,898 --> 00:34:41,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Should you be rejecting that even more?

508
00:34:41,681 --> 00:34:41,821
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

509
00:34:42,202 --> 00:34:45,968
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to accept that these possibilities are there, present, and you have no say in it.

510
00:34:46,709 --> 00:34:51,196
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you can then stop resisting it gratuitously, and thus you approve the suffering.

511
00:34:55,553 --> 00:35:00,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Or should you then accept the prospect of future pleasure that you're already delighting in?

512
00:35:00,236 --> 00:35:00,376
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

513
00:35:00,496 --> 00:35:01,257
[SPEAKER_00]: You must reject it.

514
00:35:01,297 --> 00:35:02,838
[SPEAKER_00]: You must contemplate the danger in it.

515
00:35:03,538 --> 00:35:04,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you know what you do?

516
00:35:05,300 --> 00:35:08,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by learning how to be properly mindful of an enduring feeling.

517
00:35:08,982 --> 00:35:10,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where things converge.

518
00:35:10,383 --> 00:35:11,344
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where things are rooted.

519
00:35:13,165 --> 00:35:18,088
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just take a set of behavior that you always do this when it's this and this when it's that.

520
00:35:18,849 --> 00:35:18,989
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

521
00:35:20,215 --> 00:35:27,316
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you just need to remain mindful of what's the present enduring feeling, and you will know then, what attitude is there.

522
00:35:28,056 --> 00:35:34,497
[SPEAKER_00]: If the present enduring feeling is of pleasure and agreeability, means you have the attitude of welcoming it, by default.

523
00:35:34,878 --> 00:35:35,438
[SPEAKER_01]: But you must...

524
00:35:35,958 --> 00:35:37,578
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's... that's what you reject.

525
00:35:37,978 --> 00:35:40,779
[SPEAKER_00]: You reject your welcoming of this central pleasure.

526
00:35:41,819 --> 00:35:45,359
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't blame, like giants, the actual object of your central pleasure.

527
00:35:47,240 --> 00:35:49,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So you will know what attitude is there,

528
00:35:50,719 --> 00:35:53,421
[SPEAKER_00]: By correctly understanding what present feeling is there.

529
00:35:55,563 --> 00:35:57,704
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of the problem.

530
00:35:57,804 --> 00:35:58,685
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always present.

531
00:35:58,925 --> 00:36:00,366
[SPEAKER_00]: The root of the problem is always present.

532
00:36:00,426 --> 00:36:01,207
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in the past.

533
00:36:01,267 --> 00:36:01,987
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not be in the future.

534
00:36:02,007 --> 00:36:03,228
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the root of the problem.

535
00:36:03,468 --> 00:36:04,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Attitude.

536
00:36:04,089 --> 00:36:04,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

537
00:36:04,789 --> 00:36:09,333
[SPEAKER_00]: And the root, that present root, is basically your attitude towards the presently enduring feeling.

538
00:36:10,115 --> 00:36:15,318
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's whatever suffering will arise in the future is always... That's the gateway.

539
00:36:15,358 --> 00:36:15,918
[SPEAKER_00]: It's within that.

540
00:36:15,998 --> 00:36:17,959
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't disappear when the suffering arrives.

541
00:36:17,979 --> 00:36:21,961
[SPEAKER_00]: That remains its vessel, so to speak, its framework, as I say.

542
00:36:25,202 --> 00:36:32,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Because no amount of future discomfort and suffering can affect you, unless you're holding on to that vessel of rejecting it.

543
00:36:32,746 --> 00:36:35,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the vessel of rejection, basically, of any discomfort.

544
00:36:35,567 --> 00:36:36,468
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your mindset.

545
00:36:37,550 --> 00:36:42,252
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anything that comes of discomfortable nature, it's going to make you suffer because of your attitude of not wanting it.

546
00:36:43,433 --> 00:36:55,658
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove the attitude of that gratuitous attitude of not wanting the discomfort, not necessarily seeking it out, but certainly not trying to get rid of it, when it when it's there, exactly, when it's circumstantial arise, which inevitably will.

547
00:36:56,479 --> 00:37:04,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, then you're not cultivating that gratuitous mindset of resistance, which means your craving is now truly diminishing.

548
00:37:05,183 --> 00:37:05,503
[SPEAKER_00]: And same,

549
00:37:07,200 --> 00:37:16,204
[SPEAKER_00]: You will diminish your craving when you don't cultivate Gacchuri's mindset of welcoming, as the Buddha would say, delighting, entertaining the notions of current and future pleasure.

550
00:37:17,324 --> 00:37:21,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why you... like, when you ask yourself, am I liable to future essential desire?

551
00:37:22,166 --> 00:37:22,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

552
00:37:22,787 --> 00:37:23,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

553
00:37:23,367 --> 00:37:26,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Because here and now, I'm still holding it dear.

554
00:37:26,788 --> 00:37:28,689
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm still welcoming the prospect of it.

555
00:37:29,229 --> 00:37:31,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Here and now, I haven't uprooted it.

556
00:37:31,190 --> 00:37:35,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, I haven't dealt with it rightly once it has arisen.

557
00:37:36,278 --> 00:37:36,458
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

558
00:37:37,158 --> 00:37:46,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the level of... that possible future sensuality is only possible because, right here, right now, I am still valuing it.

559
00:37:47,120 --> 00:37:49,921
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm still... haven't seen the thorough danger in it.

560
00:37:50,061 --> 00:37:52,602
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm still, on some level, delighting in it.

561
00:37:52,882 --> 00:37:58,483
[SPEAKER_00]: And that delight is to be dealt with, here and now, not in the future when that thing arises.

